<article class="env-definition" id="02I9">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02I9">Definition <span data-tag="02I9">5.20.1</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a topological space. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Let $x, y \in X$, $x \not= y$. Suppose $x \leadsto y$, that is $y$ is a specialization of $x$. We say $y$ is an <em>immediate specialization</em> of $x$ if there is no $z \in X \setminus \{ x, y\} $ with $x \leadsto z$ and $z \leadsto y$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A map $\delta : X \to \mathbf{Z}$ is called a <em>dimension function</em><a class="footnotemark" href="#a0000037538" id="a0000037538-mark"><sup>1</sup></a> if 
</p>
<ol>
  <li><p>
whenever $x \leadsto y$ and $x \not= y$ we have $\delta (x) > \delta (y)$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for every immediate specialization $x \leadsto y$ in $X$ we have $\delta (x) = \delta (y) + 1$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>